Markdown written as a string, parsed while the program builds.

`md.document(source=…)` is the hand-written door to the tree the `md:` scheme
writes for a file: the same goldmark parses it, through the build intrinsic
`md.parse`, and what comes back is data the declaration's own components lay
out -- `markup` flows for prose and code, rows for a list and a table. The
source here is a literal, so the first optimize folds the parse and every
target holds the blocks as a constant: html writes them out as markup, and
bubbletea loops over them.

A list and a table are laid out by `sngl:ui/markup/md`'s components rather
than `markup.listItem` and `ui.table`, both of which keep state of their own
and would be built at run time in the loop every block stands in.

deny out/none/html/index.html `innerHTML` -- a document is a component tree; nothing is handed to a parser at run time
deny out/none/html/index.html `__cf_` -- every block is markup the page holds, not a component the script builds
deny out/none/html/index.html `_parse` -- the parse is the build's; the script never calls it

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import md "sngl:ui/markup/md"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

const guide = "# Guide\n\nSome *emphasis*, **weight** and `code`, with [a link](/x).\n\n- one\n- two\n  1. nested\n- [x] done\n\n```go\nx := 1\n```\n\n| a | b |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | 2 |\n"

ui.window(title="guide") {
    md.document(source=guide)
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
